    "q-279456": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279456",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ given above represents a rotation of \n{{field-1}}\nradians clockwise about the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta >0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta <0$ we must have that $\\theta $ lies in $4$th quadrant. \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-1.\n$$\n\nThe principal value $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians *clockwise* about the origin.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
